  {
   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "With se, another verb",
     "texto": [
      "- **ir** is to go somewhere; **irse** is to leave: voy al cine; **me voy**.\n- **dormir** is to sleep; **dormirse** is to fall asleep.\n- **quedar** is to arrange to meet, or to be left (quedan dos); **quedarse** is to stay.\n- **acordar** is to agree; **acordarse de** is to remember.\n- **parecer** is to seem; **parecerse a** is to look like.\n- **llevar** is to carry or wear; **llevarse** is to take away, and **llevarse bien** is to get on well.\n- **despedir** is to sack; **despedirse** is to say goodbye.\n- **negar** is to deny; **negarse a** is to refuse.\n- **encontrar** is to find; **encontrarse** is to meet, or to feel.\n\nWith eating, drinking and reading, **se** shows that a whole amount is finished: **se comió** toda la tarta; **me leí** el libro en un día. It needs a definite amount: como pan, but **me comí** el pan."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "gramatica",
     "titulo": "Pairs with and without «se»",
     "texto": [
      "**Many verbs change meaning with «se»:**\n\n- ir → irse (to leave)\n- dormir → dormirse (to fall asleep)\n- quedar (to meet) → quedarse (to stay)\n- acordar (to agree) → acordarse de (to remember)\n- parecer → parecerse a (to look like)\n- despedir (to sack) → despedirse de (to say goodbye)\n- negar → negarse a (to refuse)\n\nWith eating, drinking and reading verbs, «se» shows that the whole amount is finished."
     ],
     "ejemplos": [
